WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.675
With docker run, you give an order, and Docker carries it out once.

2
00:00:06.900 --> 00:00:11.333
If that container crashes at three in the morning, nothing starts it again.

3
00:00:11.333 --> 00:00:12.929
The order was already done.

4
00:00:13.733 --> 00:00:16.086
Kubernetes works the other way round.

5
00:00:16.086 --> 00:00:18.376
You describe the end state you want.

6
00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:23.699
Kubernetes stores that description as an object, a record in its database.

7
00:00:23.699 --> 00:00:25.973
Run three copies of hello-devops.

8
00:00:26.200 --> 00:00:31.497
On the other side is the actual state: what is really running, as the nodes report it.

9
00:00:31.700 --> 00:00:35.097
Between them sits a small program called a controller.

10
00:00:35.097 --> 00:00:37.047
It runs the same loop, forever.

11
00:00:37.267 --> 00:00:39.023
Read the desired state.

12
00:00:39.233 --> 00:00:41.075
Observe the actual state.

13
00:00:41.300 --> 00:00:44.214
If the two differ, act to make them match.

14
00:00:44.214 --> 00:00:45.047
Then repeat.

15
00:00:46.867 --> 00:00:49.647
Say someone deletes one copy by mistake.

16
00:00:49.867 --> 00:00:54.033
On its next pass, the loop reads three wanted, and counts two running.

17
00:00:54.267 --> 00:00:55.664
So it starts one more.

18
00:00:55.664 --> 00:00:57.949
Nobody wrote a script for that case.

19
00:00:58.567 --> 00:01:00.330
Now you change your wish.

20
00:01:00.330 --> 00:01:02.164
Three copies becomes five.

21
00:01:02.367 --> 00:01:06.107
The loop sees five wanted and three running, and acts again.

22
00:01:06.333 --> 00:01:07.952
Two more copies start.

23
00:01:07.952 --> 00:01:09.350
You never said how.

24
00:01:09.350 --> 00:01:10.748
You only said what.

25
00:01:11.367 --> 00:01:14.115
The Kubernetes docs compare it to a thermostat.

26
00:01:14.115 --> 00:01:17.507
You set twenty-one degrees, and it keeps reading the room.

27
00:01:17.507 --> 00:01:19.963
You never tell it to heat for ten minutes.

28
00:01:20.167 --> 00:01:25.316
In every object you write, spec is your wish, and status is Kubernetes' report.

29
00:01:25.316 --> 00:01:29.097
Controllers work, all the time, to make status match spec.
